Hello all,

BrianH reports the following regarding Chris Menno tactics vis-=E0-vis Phil=
mac:

"Chris Menno's tactic for going against our Philmac is that they should hav=
e to use stiffners and Don Clark showed his service tee that had been insta=
lled on a piece of pipe and pointed out that the inside of the pipe had bee=
n deformed due to the Philmac service tee.  Don said he didn't know why the=
 Philmac doesn't need stiffners, and then went into all the independent tes=
ting that he is done and that he is very comfortable with our fitting.   Me=
nno said it doesn't make sense why our valve need stiffners and Philmac doe=
s not.  I personally need a better explanation as to why we don't need stif=
fners so I can be better at explaining this."

See Info Blurt #20 for a previous treatment of this topic.

Per Brian's request the short form explanation should be as follows:

"Harco has not qualified the use of our restraints without stiffeners there=
fore they must be used.  Philmac and other compression fitting manufacturer=
s have qualified their restraints without stiffeners and more importantly c=
onfirmed that qualification with decades of successful service that reach b=
ack to the 1960's.  It may very well be that our small diameter restraints =
could be used without stiffeners but we have not proven it therefore we are=
 obligated to require their use.  Note that the Harco restraint design is d=
ifferent than the Philmac restraint design in the details.  Harco prefers t=
o know something will work rather than hope it will work so we require the =
use of stiffeners with our restraints."

In effect this will do judo on Menno's approach.  The real question isn't w=
hy doesn't the Philmac require stiffeners but rather why can't the LIV be u=
sed without stiffeners.

Given he and undoubtedly Zack are actively using this tactic we ask that yo=
u preempt them by proactively raising the seeming inconsistency and explain=
ing it to your customers ahead of any contact from our competitors rather t=
han in reaction to that contact.

Call me for further explanation.

Background follows.

We use stiffeners because we cannot prove that they are not needed.  Harco'=
s policy is to know something will work not hope it is going to work.  Test=
imony from other joint restraint manufacturers in the US indicate they have=
 had failures without stiffeners.  Our restraint design is a direct copy of=
 the Ford/Uniflange restraint.  The long term mechanisms that might cause t=
he long term failures are not understood in such a way that short term qual=
ification tests can be formulated.  I have tested our 2" knuckle restraint =
without stiffener at elevated pressures for two years without failure.  Bec=
ause I cannot relate this short term performance to assurance of long term =
performance I shut down the test.  It was a "no go" test not a "go" test.  =
 Since we don't have time to execute a long term qualification test at actu=
al service conditions, let's say a 50 year test, it is practical to instead=
 just require a stiffener.  I believe that our 2" restraint or something li=
ke it might give good service without a stiffener but it is not worth the e=
ffort to develop it given the availability of the stiffener solution.

This approach drove us to design a low cost easy to install stiffener.  The=
 cost is so low and the market is such that we can make good profit on this=
 item.  The installation is so easy that there is no labor cost for practic=
al purposes.  If push came to shove our price for this item could be reduce=
d to the point that it would not have a bearing on a commercial outcome in =
the context of how we go to market..  We have a defensible position both te=
chnically and competitively but unfortunately seemingly inconsistent with t=
he Philmac product.

Philmac and other compression fitting makers have a restraint experience ba=
seline at this point that extends back several decades (to the 1960's).  I =
do not understand exactly why theirs works other than it does according to =
them.  They have never been able to explain to me why their system doesn't =
require one other than to refer to this long experience base line and what =
I view as arbitrary qualification tests.  They then go on to say that if so=
meone wants to use a stiffener it would be perfectly acceptable.

I suspect that there is a scale effect involved meaning as you go up in dia=
meter the restraint without stiffener becomes more problematic.  That is to=
 say the small diameters serviced by compression fittings operate in a more=
 favorable regime.  The experience of US joint restraint makers is with lar=
ger diameters.  There could also be some micro geometry effects and differe=
nces in the way the metallic grip ring responds versus the way the plastic =
grip ring responds.  It may be that our 2" grip ring doesn't require a stif=
fener (this probably would depend on DR by the way) but I cannot prove it. =
 I have thought at times about a valve with the Philmac compression end.  I=
n the end our decision to require a stiffener is driven by commercial risk =
management due to our technical ignorance.  We are wary about not knowing w=
hat we don't know.

I wish I had been more wary about the need for an inserted seat in our valv=
e.


